WEBVTT
TITLE Comparator Code Converter Bcd Adder — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
Three design problems: compare two words, re-spell a word, and add two decimal digits with a four-bit adder.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:16.000
The naive method is a truth table, and it explodes: two bits need 16 rows, eight bits need 65536.

3
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:23.000
Each problem is solved by one small quantity that summarises the bits already seen.

4
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:30.000
This is the carry-look-ahead idea wearing three different hats.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:38.000
One bit against one bit has four rows and three outputs: greater, equal, less.

6
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:46.000
Greater is a AND NOT b; equal is a XNOR b; less is NOT a AND b.

7
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:53.000
The G, E and L columns hold exactly one 1 in every row — never two, never none.

8
00:00:53.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Equal is the output that gets passed forward, because it is the only one that says "not decided yet".

9
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:08.000
Comparing 1011 with 1001 starts tied, at the most significant bit.

10
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:16.000
Bit 3 is 1 against 1, so the state stays tied and the question falls through to bit 2.

11
00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:24.000
Bit 1 is 1 against 0, so greater goes high and bit 0 is never consulted.

12
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:30.000
Checked against numeric comparison on every pair up to six bits: the ripple and the relation never disagree.

13
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:38.000
Of the 4 to the n ordered pairs, exactly 2 to the n tie.

14
00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:46.000
The rest split evenly, so greater is (4 to the n minus 2 to the n) over 2 — 120 at four bits.

15
00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:54.000
Two four-bit comparators compare eight bits, and the low stage feeds the high stage.

16
00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.000
The bottom stage must start tied: greater 0, equal 1, less 0.

17
00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:08.000
A code is a spelling: the same digit, written in a different four-bit word.

18
00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:16.000
BCD writes each decimal digit in four bits, so ten of the sixteen words have a meaning.

19
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:23.000
Excess-3 adds three, and inverting its bits gives the code for the nines complement.

20
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Every row of the table confirms it: the inverted code equals the code for 9 minus the digit.

21
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:38.000
Binary to Gray: the top bit is copied, and every lower Gray bit is that binary bit exclusive-or the one above.

22
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:46.000
1011 becomes 1110: g3 is 1, g2 is 1 xor 0, g1 is 0 xor 1, g0 is 1 xor 1.

23
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:54.000
Down the whole list, each row differs from the one before it in exactly one bit.

24
00:02:54.000 --> 00:03:00.000
The last word and the first also differ in one bit, so the sixteen words form a cycle.

25
00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:08.000
The Karnaugh map axis is labelled 00, 01, 11, 10 — not 00, 01, 10, 11.

26
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:16.000
Two labels differing in one bit sit next to each other, so their cells can merge.

27
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:24.000
A block of four keeps two literals instead of four, because two bits are free to differ.

28
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.000
Checked: the four axis labels are the Gray sequence and every adjacent pair differs in one bit, wrap included.

29
00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:38.000
Two BCD digits plus a carry in give a binary sum anywhere from 0 to 19.

30
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:46.000
Ten to nineteen are not one decimal digit, so the answer needs a carry and a corrected nibble.

31
00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:54.000
The repair is to add six whenever the binary sum exceeds nine.

32
00:03:54.000 --> 00:04:00.000
All 200 inputs are checked against decimal addition: no disagreements.

33
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:08.000
Six is sixteen minus ten, so adding it wraps the nibble by one decimal place.

34
00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:16.000
Each of the six rescued words lands on 0 to 5 and raises the carry.

35
00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:23.000
The correction condition is C-out, or S3 S2, or S3 S1.

36
00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:30.000
Ten to fifteen really do arrive, so this is not a don't-care problem — the condition must be right on all sixteen words.

37
00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:38.000
Name the input word and the output word before writing any gate.

38
00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:46.000
Find the small quantity that summarises the lower bits, then write one rule per position.

39
00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:53.000
Trap one: comparing from the least significant bit. Trap two: a cascade input that is not a tie.

40
00:04:53.000 --> 00:05:00.000
Trap three: using the decode chain to encode. Traps four and five: treating 10 to 15 as don't-cares, and forgetting the corrected carry.

